        <div id="does-it-need-sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Does Your Granite Even Need Sealing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Granite is not one material. The trade name covers dozens of igneous and metamorphic stones with very different pore structures. Absolute Black and many dark gabbros are so dense that a sealer has nowhere to go, while some light quarries, especially pale whites and creams with visible crystals, will darken under a drop of water in under a minute. Sealing a stone that cannot absorb product does nothing except leave residue to buff off.
          </p>

          <p>
            The quickest way to find out is a water test. Drip a small puddle of water on several spots, especially near the sink and the cooktop, and watch it. If the stone under the puddle darkens within one to four minutes, it is absorbent and needs sealing. If it takes 10 to 30 minutes, it is moderately absorbent and one coat will help. If nothing changes after half an hour, the slab is dense and sealing is optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a second test with cooking oil, because some stones resist water but still wick up oil. Put a few drops of vegetable oil on an inconspicuous spot, wait 15 minutes, then wipe. A dark ring that does not wipe away means oil is getting in, and a fluoropolymer or high-performance impregnator is worth paying for. A clean spot means a standard sealer is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lemon juice is a third test some fabricators use, but it is for marble and limestone contamination, not granite absorption. True granite will not etch from lemon juice. If a counter sold as granite goes dull under lemon juice, it probably contains calcite and should be treated like marble; see our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">marble sealer guide</a> for how that changes the advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Solvent, Water or Fluoropolymer: What Is in the Bottle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product on this list is an impregnator, meaning it soaks into the stone and lines the pores rather than forming a coating on top. Film-forming sealers such as acrylics and polyurethanes are wrong for countertops: they scratch, peel and leave a plastic look. If the label says topical, coating or high-gloss, it does not belong on a kitchen counter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based impregnators use mineral spirits or a similar carrier. The carrier is thin, so it pulls active ingredients deeper into tight stone, then evaporates. The downside is odor and flammability during application. Water-based impregnators use water as the carrier, which is safer and nearly odorless, but water has higher surface tension and may not travel as far into very dense granite. Modern water-based products have closed most of that gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            The active ingredients matter more than the carrier. Silicone and siloxane resins repel water well and oil moderately. Fluoropolymer resins, often called fluorochemical or fluorocarbon sealers, repel both water and oil and are the reason premium products cost more. In a kitchen where olive oil, butter and bacon grease are daily hazards, oil repellency is the feature that pays off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Color-enhancing sealers combine an impregnator with an ingredient that saturates the stone and darkens it. They are a cosmetic choice. For the broader picture of penetrating chemistries, including the silane and siloxane families used on concrete and masonry, see our explainer on <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane sealers</a>.
          </p>

