        <div id="match-to-stone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Matching the Sealer to Your Slab
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Dense dark granite</strong>, such as Absolute Black, Uba Tuba and Black Pearl, rarely needs more than a light maintenance product, and often nothing at all. Overapplying here is the most common cause of hazy streaks. If the water test shows almost no absorption, a spray like Granite Gold once a year is the most you should do.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Medium-porosity granite</strong>, the large middle group of browns, golds and speckled patterns, is where a standard impregnator such as Miracle 511 or StoneTech BulletProof earns its keep. One full coat after installation and then a reseal every one to three years, guided by the water test, is a sensible schedule for most of these slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Light and open-grained granite</strong>, including many whites, creams and stones with large crystals or visible fissures, can absorb a surprising amount. These are the slabs that show oil rings and coffee stains. Use a premium oil-repelling sealer, apply two coats, and test again after a week. Some of these quarries are resin-treated at the factory, which reduces absorption, but the resin does not cover every pore.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Honed, leathered and flamed finishes</strong> are always more absorbent than the polished version of the same stone because the finishing process opens the surface. They also show oil and water marks more readily. Seal them more generously, and consider a color enhancer if the finish looks washed out. For granite outside, the same logic applies as for any exterior masonry; our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers how weather exposure changes product choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seams deserve a separate look. The epoxy or polyester filler in a countertop seam is not stone and does not need sealer, but the stone edges on either side of it often absorb more than the field because they were cut and polished on site. Give the first inch either side of each seam, the sink cutout rim and the cooktop cutout an extra pass of sealer, since water and oil collect at those edges first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Seal Granite Without Leaving Haze
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a clean, completely dry surface. Clean with a pH-neutral stone cleaner, not vinegar or bleach, and remove any stains first, because sealer locks in whatever is already in the pores. Then let the counter dry for at least 24 hours, longer if it was recently wet around the sink. Sealing damp stone traps moisture and blocks penetration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in sections of about 3 by 3 feet. Apply the sealer with a lint-free cloth, foam brush or the spray nozzle so the surface looks evenly wet. Let it dwell for the time on the label, usually around 5 to 15 minutes, while keeping it wet; add a little more to spots that soak up product fast. That dwell period is when the sealer actually enters the stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next comes the step that decides how the job looks: wipe off every bit of excess. Use clean dry cloths and buff until the surface feels like bare stone with no tackiness. Any sealer left on top will dry into a hazy, streaky film that is harder to remove later. Change cloths often. If a second coat is needed, wait the interval the label gives, typically 20 minutes to a few hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most impregnators reach handling strength in a few hours, but full cure usually takes 24 to 72 hours depending on the product. Keep water, oil and acidic food off the surface during that time, and do not run a wet test until the cure window has passed. If a haze appears the next day, rub it with a little more of the same sealer on a cloth, which re-dissolves the residue, then buff dry immediately.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Nothing should dry on the surface</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">An impregnator only works inside the stone. Whatever is left on top after the dwell time is waste that will cure into haze. Wipe until the cloth comes away dry and the counter feels like stone again.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Sealer You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage depends far more on the stone than on the product. A dense dark granite may take a quart over 300 to 600 square feet, while a thirsty light granite can use the same quart on 100 to 200 square feet. The data sheet range for each product reflects that spread, so start in the middle and adjust after you see how quickly your slab drinks.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical kitchen has 30 to 60 square feet of counter, counting the island. Even on porous stone, that is well inside one quart for two coats. Buying a gallon for a kitchen is a waste unless you are also sealing a stone floor, a backsplash and bathroom vanities. Most sealers keep for a few years if capped tightly, so a leftover quart can serve the next reseal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outdoor kitchens, granite pavers and stone steps are a different calculation. Flamed exterior granite absorbs more, and you are covering more area, so gallon sizes make sense there. Measure the total square footage and divide by the low end of the coverage range on the label to avoid running short in the middle of a section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget roughly $15 to $90 for a countertop job depending on the product tier, versus $150 to $300 or more for a professional sealing visit. The do-it-yourself job takes about an hour of work plus the drying wait. There is no real skill barrier as long as you follow the wipe-off step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Keeping the Seal Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer wears out mainly through cleaning, not through use. Harsh cleaners strip it far faster than food or water. Vinegar, lemon-based sprays, bleach, ammonia and many all-purpose bathroom cleaners are either acidic or strongly alkaline, and they break down impregnators over time. Use a pH-neutral stone cleaner or a few drops of mild dish soap in warm water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wipe spills promptly, especially oil, wine, coffee and anything with strong pigment such as turmeric or beet juice. A sealer slows absorption; it does not make stone stain-proof. On a well-sealed slab, you typically have minutes to hours to wipe something up before it penetrates, which is plenty for normal kitchen life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recheck the counter with the water test once a year, focusing on the areas around the sink and stove where wear is highest. When water starts darkening the stone within about five minutes, it is time to reseal. Many dense counters go three to five years or longer between treatments; porous slabs might need attention annually.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a stain does get in, a poultice can usually pull it out. For oil, mix baking soda with a little acetone or a dedicated stone poultice powder into a paste, cover it with plastic, let it sit 24 to 48 hours, then remove. Reseal the spot afterward, since the poultice also draws out sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water rings are a different case from stains. If a glass leaves a dark ring that disappears after an hour or two, that is just water evaporating out of the stone, and it means the seal near that spot is getting thin. A ring that stays light or dull is mineral deposit from hard water, which comes off with a neutral cleaner and a soft pad. Neither is permanent, but both are a useful early signal that resealing is due.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outdoor-granite" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Sealing Granite Outside
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoor granite, from kitchen islands to pool coping and stair treads, faces sun, rain, freeze-thaw cycles, sprinklers and organic stains from leaves and berries. Most exterior granite has a flamed or thermal finish for slip resistance, which leaves a rougher, more porous surface than a polished kitchen slab. That means it absorbs more sealer and more staining agents.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a sealer rated explicitly for exterior use. Water-repellent impregnators remain breathable, which matters outside: moisture that gets into stone from below or from the edges must be able to escape as vapor. A film-forming coating traps that moisture and can lead to spalling or white efflorescence deposits in cold climates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean exterior granite thoroughly before sealing, ideally with a stone-safe cleaner and a low-pressure rinse rather than a high-pressure blast, which can etch soft mineral veins. Let it dry for a full 48 hours of dry weather. Apply in mild temperatures, typically between 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, and avoid direct midday sun, which can flash the carrier off before the sealer penetrates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect to reseal outdoor granite every one to three years. Granite pavers set in sand behave much like other paver systems; our <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer guide</a> covers joint sand and edge sealing if your granite is part of a patio.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Granite Sealer Food-Safe?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once cured, most reputable impregnating sealers are considered safe for food-contact countertops. The active ingredients sit inside the stone, and the solvents or water carriers evaporate completely during cure. Several manufacturers state on their data sheets that the cured product is safe for food preparation surfaces; check the specific label or safety data sheet if this matters to you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The risk is during application and cure, not afterward. Solvent-based products release volatile organic compounds and have flammable vapors, so turn off pilot lights and do not run gas burners near the work area, ventilate well, and keep children and pets out of the room until the smell is gone. Water-based products are far milder but still need the cure time on the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wait the full cure period before putting food directly on the counter, typically 24 to 72 hours. Until then, use a cutting board or keep food prep elsewhere. After cure, wash the surface once with a neutral stone cleaner before normal use.
          </p>

          <p>
            If anyone in the house is sensitive to fumes, choose a water-based sealer such as StoneTech BulletProof or Granite Gold and schedule the job for a day when the windows can stay open. The trade-off in protection is small, and you can always reapply sooner.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pro-or-diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Hiring a Pro vs Doing It Yourself
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fabricators often seal a new counter at installation, but not always, and what they use varies from premium fluoropolymers to whatever is cheapest by the gallon. Ask the installer which product went on and when. If they cannot tell you, run the water test a week after install and decide for yourself rather than assuming the job was done.
          </p>

          <p>
            A professional stone-care company makes sense when the counter also needs work a sealer cannot do: polishing out etch marks, removing a deep stain, filling a chip or pit, or restoring a dull honed finish. Those visits usually include sealing at the end, and the combined service is better value than paying separately for a sealing-only call.
          </p>

          <p>
            For straightforward resealing, doing it yourself wins on cost and timing. A homeowner with a quart of good impregnator and a few cloths gets the same result as a technician using the same chemistry, because the product does the work. The only real skill is wiping off the excess thoroughly, which takes patience rather than training.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you rent or plan to sell, keep a note of the product and date of each sealing. Buyers and landlords increasingly ask about stone maintenance, and a short record shows the counters were cared for. It also tells you, three years from now, which bottle worked well on that specific slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stain-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Common Countertop Stains and How to Lift Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Oil and grease</strong> leave a dark, spreading shadow that often looks like the stone is permanently wet. Use a poultice made with an absorbent powder and acetone or a commercial oil-lifting poultice, cover it with plastic, and let it work for a day or two. Repeat if the shadow fades but does not disappear; deep oil stains can take several rounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Coffee, tea, wine and food dyes</strong> are organic stains that usually show as brown or reddish marks. On light granite, a poultice made with 12 percent hydrogen peroxide and a little ammonia or a commercial organic-stain poultice often lifts them. Test on a hidden spot first, since peroxide can lighten some dark stones.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Rust stains</strong> from cast-iron pans or metal cans are orange-brown and stubborn. They need a rust remover made for natural stone; ordinary rust removers are often acidic and can damage stones containing calcite. If the stain is old and deep, a stone restoration professional may be the better option.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hard-water marks</strong> around faucets are mineral deposits sitting on the surface, not stains in the stone. A neutral stone cleaner and a soft nylon pad usually remove them. After any stain removal, let the area dry for a day and reseal it, because poultices pull sealer out along with the stain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing stone that cannot absorb anything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dense granites such as Absolute Black will not take sealer. Applying it anyway leaves a sticky, streaky film that has to be stripped with solvent. Do the water test first, and if water still sits on the surface after half an hour without darkening it, skip the sealer and save the money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting excess sealer dry on top</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The product only works inside the stone. Anything left on the surface after the dwell time cures into a dull haze that shows in raking light. Wipe and buff with clean dry cloths until the counter feels like bare stone, and change cloths as soon as they get damp.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over a stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer locks in whatever is already in the pores. If there is an oil ring, a coffee spot or a water mark, poultice it out first and let the stone dry fully for a day or two. Sealing over it makes the stain permanent and also blocks the poultice from working later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning with vinegar or bleach</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acidic and harsh alkaline cleaners break down impregnators and shorten the life of the seal dramatically. They can also dull polished finishes on stones that contain any calcite, which many slabs sold as granite do. Switch to a pH-neutral stone cleaner or mild dish soap and water for daily wiping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a topical coating meant for concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acrylic and urethane coatings designed for driveways or garage floors form a film that scratches, yellows and peels on a countertop. They also make the stone look plastic and are miserable to strip off later. Use only impregnating sealers rated for natural stone on any surface that sees food prep.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the right resealing interval for a granite counter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no fixed schedule because granite varies so much. Use the water test once a year: if a puddle darkens the stone within about five minutes, reseal. Dense dark granites may go five years or more, or never need it. Light, porous slabs might need sealing every year. Areas near the sink and stove usually wear first, so test those spots.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is sealing a counter a reasonable DIY job?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Sealing granite is one of the easiest stone-care jobs a homeowner can do. You need a clean, dry counter, the sealer, and a stack of lint-free cloths. Apply, wait the dwell time on the label, and buff off all excess. The whole job usually takes under an hour plus cure time, and it costs a fraction of a professional visit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a clear impregnator alter color or shine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard impregnating sealer should not change color or sheen once excess is wiped off. Color-enhancing sealers are the exception: they deliberately darken the stone and bring out the pattern, and the effect is largely permanent. If a regular sealer leaves the counter looking cloudy, that is residue on the surface, not a change to the stone itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it a problem to leave a slab untreated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On dense stone, very little. On porous stone, oils, wine and coffee can soak in and leave dark stains that need poulticing. Unsealed granite does not crack or fall apart from lack of sealing. The risk is cosmetic staining, so the value of sealing depends entirely on how absorbent your particular slab is.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is granite sealer the same as granite cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Cleaner removes dirt and grease from the surface. Sealer soaks into the stone and lines the pores to slow absorption. Some combination products clean and leave a trace of sealer behind, which helps maintain an existing seal but is not a substitute for a proper sealing on a porous slab. Use a dedicated sealer for the main application.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can use the counter after sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most impregnators allow light use within a few hours, but full cure usually takes 24 to 72 hours. During that time keep water, oil and acidic foods off the surface and avoid setting anything wet on it. Check your product label for its specific cure window, and wait until it passes before judging the result with a water test.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealer fix etching or dull spots on granite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. True granite rarely etches. If dull spots appear where acidic foods sat, the stone likely contains calcite, which etches like marble. Sealer cannot prevent or repair that. Etch marks are removed with a polishing compound or by a stone restoration professional, and the area should be resealed afterward because polishing removes the old sealer.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

